Package: DSEMr
Title: Bayesian Dynamic Structural Equation Modeling
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R: c(
    person("Marcus", "Harris", role = c("aut", "cre"),
           email = "marcus.harris.uconn@gmail.com"),
    person("The extendr contributors", role = "ctb",
           comment = "Authors of vendored Rust crates; see inst/AUTHORS")
  )
Description: Specifies, simulates, and estimates Bayesian dynamic structural
    equation models for intensive longitudinal and time-series data using a
    compiled 'Rust' numerical backend and an R reference engine. Models can be
    expressed with an extension of 'lavaan' syntax or standard section-based
    SEM scripts and compile to a unified intermediate representation. Supports
    single-level vector autoregressive, residual dynamic (Asparouhov and
    Muthen, 2020, <doi:10.1080/10705511.2019.1626733>), continuous-time
    Ornstein-Uhlenbeck (Oud and Jansen, 2000), dynamic factor, white-noise
    factor score, binary or ordinal probit, and Tobit censored models;
    two-level location-scale, exact collapsed ARMA(1,1), and multivariate
    dynamic models with latent variable centering, random slopes, random log
    innovation variances, random residual covariances, correlated within-level
    innovations, and between-level structural regressions (Asparouhov,
    Hamaker, and Muthen, 2018, <doi:10.1080/10705511.2017.1406803>; McNeish
    and Hamaker, 2020, <doi:10.1037/met0000250>); three-level dynamic
    structural equation models with simultaneous two-level block Gibbs
    sampling, Level-2 autoregression, and three-level latent decomposition;
    cross-classified subject-by-time models and cross-classified dynamic
    factor analysis with random factor loadings; time-interval grid alignment;
    penalized shrinkage priors; cluster-averaged and cluster-specific
    standardized estimates and Bayesian R-squared (Schuurman, Ferrer, de
    Boer-Sonnenschein, and Hamaker, 2016, <doi:10.1037/met0000062>);
    posterior partial correlations, derived parameter hypothesis and Wald
    tests, discrete and continuous-time impulse response functions, cluster
    random-effect rankings, covariate variance inflation factors, Bayesian DIC
    model comparison, publication-ready APA tables, LaTeX model equations,
    visual path diagrams, reproducible code generation, deterministic parallel
    Monte Carlo simulation studies, and an optional interactive 'shiny' studio.
